# Attention, From Scratch

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1"},
  {:tiny_llm, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")}
])
```

## What this is

A complete decoder-only transformer language model in pure Elixir standard
library. No Nx, no Axon, no hex packages in the model code. Hand-derived
backpropagation. About thirteen thousand parameters, trained on a laptop CPU
in under a minute.

This notebook is the talk, in talk order. Kino and VegaLite appear here for
*presentation only*; they never touch the math.

**Built so far: stages 1 and 2.** The vocabulary, the grammar that writes our
training data, the math library, and a count-based bigram. The bigram is the
baseline the transformer has to beat, and by the end of this notebook you will
be able to see exactly where it fails and why.

```elixir
alias TinyLlm.Bigram
alias TinyLlm.Grammar
alias TinyLlm.Tensor
alias TinyLlm.Vocab
alias VegaLite, as: Vl

:ok
```

## The entire vocabulary

Thirty-two words. One word is one token is one integer. There is no
tokenizer anywhere in this project, which means there is nothing hidden
between the text you read and the numbers the model sees.

The ordering is deliberate: every word class occupies a contiguous range of
ids. That choice pays off the moment we draw a picture.

```elixir
[
  {"determiners", Vocab.determiners()},
  {"nouns", Vocab.nouns()},
  {"transitive verbs", Vocab.transitive_verbs()},
  {"intransitive verbs", Vocab.intransitive_verbs()},
  {"copula", Vocab.copula()},
  {"adjectives", Vocab.adjectives()},
  {"connectives", Vocab.connectives()},
  {"boundaries", [Vocab.start_token(), Vocab.end_token()]}
]
|> Enum.map(fn {class, words} ->
  ids = Enum.map(words, &Vocab.word_to_id/1)

  %{
    "class" => class,
    "ids" => "#{Enum.min(ids)}-#{Enum.max(ids)}",
    "words" => Enum.join(words, " ")
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(name: "32 words, grouped by class")
```

A word is only ever its id, and back again:

```elixir
sentence = ~w(the big llama sees a mouse .)

%{
  words: sentence,
  ids: Vocab.encode(sentence),
  back_again: sentence |> Vocab.encode() |> Vocab.decode()
}
```

## We own the training data

Most of what makes language models hard to study is that nobody knows what is
in the training set. So we write ours with a probabilistic grammar, which makes
the ground truth knowable: we can ask whether the model learned subject-verb
agreement, rather than guessing.

```
Sentence        -> NounPhrase VerbPhrase "."

NounPhrase      -> Determiner AdjectivePhrase Noun (70%)
                 | NounPhrase "and" NounPhrase (15%)
                 | Determiner AdjectivePhrase Noun "who" VerbPhrase (15%)

AdjectivePhrase -> nothing (55%)
                 | Adjective (30%)
                 | Adjective Adjective (15%)

VerbPhrase      -> IntransitiveVerb (35%)
                 | TransitiveVerb NounPhrase (35%)
                 | Copula Adjective (30%)
```

Agreement is enforced while sampling, so every sentence is grammatical by
construction. A singular-or-plural flag is decided once, when a noun phrase
picks its noun, then travels up to the caller and back down into the verb.

```elixir
Grammar.seed(42)

Grammar.corpus(12)
|> Enum.map_join("\n", &Enum.join(&1, " "))
|> Kino.Text.new()
```

### The sentence this whole talk is about

```
the llama who chases the dogs flees
    ^^^^^                     ^^^^^   subject and verb, both singular
                      ^^^^            distractor: plural, and nearest the verb
```

Fifteen percent of subjects take a relative clause, which drops a **distractor
noun** between the subject and its verb. The verb still has to agree with the
head noun, `llama`, and not with `dogs` sitting right beside it.

Anything predicting agreement from the nearest noun answers `flee` and is
wrong. Getting this right means knowing that `llama` is the subject and `dogs`
is merely the object of a subordinate clause. That is the entire task.

Linguists call this **agreement attraction**, and it is a standard probe for
whether a model has learned syntax or surface statistics. Humans fail it too:
"the key to the cabinets *were* rusty" is an error fluent speakers produce.

The grammar generates this shape constantly, and it is easy to find because a
plural noun immediately followed by a singular verb can only happen when that
verb agrees with something further back:

```elixir
singular_verbs =
  Vocab.transitive_verbs(:singular) ++
    Vocab.intransitive_verbs(:singular) ++ Vocab.copula(:singular)

distractor_case? = fn words ->
  words
  |> Enum.chunk_every(2, 1, :discard)
  |> Enum.any?(fn [first, second] ->
    first in Vocab.nouns(:plural) and second in singular_verbs
  end)
end

Grammar.seed(1234)

Grammar.corpus(20_000)
|> Enum.filter(distractor_case?)
|> Enum.take(8)
|> Enum.map_join("\n", &Enum.join(&1, " "))
|> Kino.Text.new()
```

Every one of those is a sentence where the last noun before the verb disagrees
with it. They are rare, about 1.3% of the corpus, which matters later: the
stage 6 evaluation has to be scored on these specifically. Score it on all
relative clauses instead and a model guessing from the nearest noun collects
roughly half the points by accident.

## A bigram: the dumbest model that works

Count which word follows which, normalize each row, sample. No parameters
learned by gradient descent, no notion of a subject, and no memory beyond one
word back.

```elixir
Grammar.seed(20_250_807)

corpus = Grammar.corpus(2_000)
counts = Bigram.counts(corpus)
matrix = Bigram.matrix(counts)

%{
  sentences: length(corpus),
  distinct_pairs_observed: map_size(counts),
  possible_pairs: Vocab.size() * Vocab.size(),
  matrix_shape: "#{length(matrix)} x #{length(hd(matrix))}"
}
```

### The heatmap

Row = the word you just saw. Column = the word that comes next. Each row is a
probability distribution, so each row sums to 1.

Both axes are the full vocabulary in id order, which is why the grammar's rules
show up as **blocks** rather than as noise.

```elixir
words = Vocab.words()

cells =
  for {row, previous_id} <- Enum.with_index(matrix),
      {probability, next_id} <- Enum.with_index(row) do
    %{
      "previous" => Vocab.id_to_word(previous_id),
      "next" => Vocab.id_to_word(next_id),
      "probability" => probability
    }
  end

Vl.new(width: 620, height: 620, title: "P(next word | previous word)")
|> Vl.data_from_values(cells)
|> Vl.mark(:rect)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "next",
  type: :nominal,
  sort: words,
  axis: [title: "next word", label_angle: -45]
)
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "previous",
  type: :nominal,
  sort: words,
  axis: [title: "previous word"]
)
|> Vl.encode_field(:color, "probability",
  type: :quantitative,
  scale: [scheme: "blues"]
)
```

### Reading it

Every bright block is a grammar rule you can point at. These are checks, not
claims:

```elixir
successors = fn word ->
  matrix
  |> Enum.at(Vocab.word_to_id(word))
  |> Enum.with_index()
  |> Enum.reject(fn {probability, _id} -> probability == 0.0 end)
  |> Enum.map(fn {_probability, id} -> Vocab.id_to_word(id) end)
  |> Enum.sort()
end

[Vocab.start_token(), "is", "are", "sees", "chases"]
|> Enum.map(fn word ->
  %{"after" => word, "can only be followed by" => Enum.join(successors.(word), " ")}
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(name: "the grammar, visible in the counts")
```

`<start>` is followed only by determiners, because every sentence begins with
one. The copula is followed only by adjectives, which is `Copula Adjective`
with nothing else possible. Transitive verbs are followed only by determiners,
because they always take an object noun phrase.

And two white stripes mark the sequence boundaries: nothing precedes `<start>`,
so its column is empty, and nothing follows `.`, so its row is empty.

```elixir
%{
  "start token column (nothing precedes a start)" =>
    matrix |> Enum.map(&Enum.at(&1, Vocab.word_to_id(Vocab.start_token()))) |> Enum.sum(),
  "period row (nothing follows an end)" =>
    matrix |> Enum.at(Vocab.word_to_id(Vocab.end_token())) |> Enum.sum(),
  "distinct sums of every other row" =>
    matrix
    |> Enum.map(&Enum.sum/1)
    |> Enum.reject(&(&1 == 0.0))
    |> Enum.map(&Float.round(&1, 12))
    |> Enum.uniq()
}
```

### Where the bigram wins

Here is the surprise. The bigram gets adjacent agreement **mostly right, for
free**, with no notion of number anywhere in it. After a singular noun it
expects a singular verb; after a plural noun, a plural one. It learned that
purely from counting.

```elixir
singular_verbs = ~w(sees chases ignores flees is)
plural_verbs = ~w(see chase ignore flee are)

verb_mass = fn word ->
  entries =
    matrix
    |> Enum.at(Vocab.word_to_id(word))
    |> Enum.with_index()
    |> Enum.map(fn {probability, id} -> {probability, Vocab.id_to_word(id)} end)

  mass = fn forms ->
    entries |> Enum.filter(fn {_p, w} -> w in forms end) |> Enum.map(&elem(&1, 0)) |> Enum.sum()
  end

  {mass.(singular_verbs), mass.(plural_verbs)}
end

~w(llama llamas dog dogs mouse mice)
|> Enum.map(fn word ->
  {singular, plural} = verb_mass.(word)
  agreeing = if word in Vocab.nouns(:plural), do: plural, else: singular

  %{
    "after" => word,
    "singular verb mass" => Float.round(singular, 3),
    "plural verb mass" => Float.round(plural, 3),
    "share that agrees" => "#{Float.round(agreeing / (singular + plural) * 100, 1)}%"
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(name: "verb mass following each noun")
```

### Where it cannot

Look at the share that *disagrees*. It is not noise, and it is not evenly
spread. It is exactly the two constructions in our grammar that break
adjacency:

* A plural verb after a **singular** noun comes from a compound subject:
  `the goose and the llama flee`. The pair `llama flee` is real.
* A singular verb after a **plural** noun comes from a relative clause:
  `the llama who chases the dogs flees`. The pair `dogs flees` is real, and
  that sentence is the distractor case itself.

So the disagreeing mass in each row is a fingerprint of the very sentences the
bigram cannot handle. Restricted to the two candidate forms, here is what it
answers after `dogs`:

```elixir
{singular, plural} = verb_mass.("dogs")

%{
  "context" => "the llama who chases the dogs ___",
  "correct answer" => "singular, agreeing with llama",
  "bigram answers plural with confidence" =>
    "#{Float.round(plural / (singular + plural) * 100, 1)}%",
  "single most likely next token overall" =>
    matrix |> Enum.at(Vocab.word_to_id("dogs")) |> Tensor.argmax() |> Vocab.id_to_word()
}
```

The bigram conditions on one word, and that word is `dogs`. It will answer
plural essentially every time, and it will be wrong essentially every time.

```
the llama who chases the dogs ___
                      ^^^^        the only thing a bigram can see
    ^^^^^                         the only thing that matters
```

It does not fail at agreement in general. It fails **precisely and only** when
a distractor sits between a subject and its verb, because there is no row in
this matrix for "the subject, four words ago."

That missing row is what attention is for.

### What it generates

Sample from the start row, follow the chain, stop at a period.

Every adjacent pair below is locally plausible, because every adjacent pair was
observed. The sentences are still nonsense, because nothing tracks a subject
for longer than one word.

```elixir
Bigram.seed(7)

1..12
|> Enum.map_join("\n", fn _draw -> matrix |> Bigram.sentence() |> Enum.join(" ") end)
|> Kino.Text.new()
```

## Still to come

The remaining stages, each of which slots in below this line:

* **Stage 3, the neural bigram.** Same one-word context, but the counts become
  learned parameters: an embedding, a projection, a softmax, and cross-entropy
  loss with a hand-derived gradient. Loss starts at `ln(32) ~ 3.466` and should
  fall below 2.
* **Stage 4, attention.** One causal self-attention head over the whole
  16-token context. Learned positional embeddings, scaled dot-product scores, a
  causal mask, and a backward pass derived by hand in `docs/backprop.md`.
* **Stage 5, the block.** Residual connections, RMSNorm, and a 32 to 128 to 32
  MLP, composed into a model.
* **Stage 6, sampling and evaluation.** A temperature knob, and the numbers
  that settle the argument: agreement accuracy for the bigram, the neural
  bigram, and the full model, measured on held-out `who`-clause sentences.
* **Stage 7, the pictures.** Embedding PCA, expecting part-of-speech clusters
  and parallel singular-to-plural offsets, plus the attention heatmap for
  `the llama who chases the dogs`, where the verb position should attend to
  `llama` and not to `dogs`.
